"""Explicit models for the recovered January28–29 mathematical archive."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
text=json.loads((P/'root_final_sweep.json').read_text())[0]['result']['structuredContent']['messages'][0]['content']
entries=[]
def add(n,title,question,definitions,statement,proof,example,limits,extension,needle,refs=[]):
    line=next(x for x in text.splitlines()if needle in x)
    date=re.search(r'A@(2026-\d\d-\d\d)',line).group(1)
    entries.append(dict(id=f'Y26-01-{900+n:03}',title=title,status='Explicit theorem and newly stipulated worked model',summary='A broad recovered archive topic is made concrete through the specified mathematical object below. The construction supplies a proof and an exact or analytically evaluated example; it does not assert that missing historical formulas have been recovered.',definitions=definitions,result=dict(name=title,statement=statement,proof=proof),example=dict(zip(['setup','calculation','conclusion'],example)),limits=limits,extension=extension,references=refs,original=dict(date=date,month='2026-01',evidence='A',field='Probability, spectral geometry and differential equations',concept=title,origin='Recovered January28–29 assistant-attributed mathematical archive topic; new explicit development.',question=question,math=statement,finish='Proof, worked example and scoped extension',kind='Revised mathematical treatment',refs=[r['url']for r in refs],source_excerpt=line,retrieval_receipt='root_final_sweep.json record 0',original_question=None,attribution_note='The source is a dated topic summary, not a verbatim user prompt. This example is newly chosen. A named general field is not treated as an already proved historical theorem.')))
add(1,'Itô correction and a Feynman–Kac model with an exact solution',
 'Which second-order term converts noisy trajectories into a backward differential equation, and can one check it on a complete example?',
 ['Let X solve dX_t=b(X_t)dt+σ(X_t)dW_t and let Lf=bf′+σ²f″/2. Assume enough coefficient regularity for a nonexplosive strong solution and enough smoothness and integrability for the Itô formulas and expectations used below.',
 'For bounded V, a classical solution u_t+Lu−Vu=0 with terminal value u(T,x)=g(x) is considered. Assume the stopped local martingales are uniformly integrable, or impose bounds that guarantee this.'],
 'u(t,x)=E_x[exp(−∫_t^T V(X_s)ds)g(X_T)] whenever the stated martingale and classical-solution hypotheses hold. In particular, for dX=−κXdt+σdW, κ>0, V=0 and g(x)=x², u(t,x)=e^(−2κτ)x²+σ²(1−e^(−2κτ))/(2κ), τ=T−t.',
 'Itô expansion gives df(X_t)=f′(X_t)dX_t+f″(X_t)d[X]_t/2, with d[X]_t=σ(X_t)²dt. Apply the time-dependent formula and the product rule to M_s=exp(−∫_t^s V(X_r)dr)u(s,X_s). Its drift vanishes exactly when the displayed PDE holds. Localization and the stated uniform-integrability condition turn M into a true martingale; evaluating at t and T proves the representation. For the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck example, Itô gives d(X²)=(−2κX²+σ²)dt+2σX dW. Its expected value solves m′=−2κm+σ² with m(0)=x², producing the explicit formula. Direct differentiation of u verifies u_t−κxu_x+σ²u_xx/2=0 and the terminal condition, so probability and PDE agree without a numerical solver.',
 ['Take κ=1,σ=√2,x=2 andτ=log2.','The moment is u=4e^(−2log2)+1−e^(−2log2)=7/4. Omitting the Itô correction would incorrectly predict1.','The additional3/4 is the accumulated noise variance, obtained exactly from the quadratic-variation term.'],
 'This is a linear diffusion example and a conditional classical Feynman–Kac argument. It does not assert existence of a classical PDE solution under merely measurable coefficients or permit ignoring uniform integrability.',
 'Extend the construction to a specified bounded killing potential and prove both existence and uniqueness in a stated growth class, including an explicit stopping/localization estimate.',
 'Middle-layer equations:')
add(2,'The semicircle law has exact Catalan moments before any random-matrix limit',
 'What does the stated semicircle density itself imply, independently of an unproved claim about a matrix ensemble?',
 ['Define the probability density ρ(x)=√(4−x²)/(2π) on[−2,2], zero outside. Let m_j=∫x^jρ(x)dx.',
 'For |z|>2 let G(z)=∫ρ(x)/(z−x)dx. Choose the branch of√(z²−4) that behaves like z at infinity.'],
 'm_{2k}=(1/(k+1))binom(2k,k), m_{2k+1}=0, and G(z)=(z−√(z²−4))/2. Consequently G²−zG+1=0 and G(z)∼1/z.',
 'Symmetry gives the odd moments. Substitute x=2√t on the positive half of the even-moment integral. One gets m_{2k}=2^(2k+1)B(k+1/2,3/2)/π. Using Γ(3/2)=√π/2 and Γ(k+1/2)=(2k)!√π/(4^k k!) reduces this to the Catalan number. In particular m_0=1, so normalization is proved rather than assumed. For |z|>2, expand1/(z−x) geometrically and interchange with the integral by uniform absolute convergence. Thus G(z)=Σ_{k≥0}C_k z^(−2k−1). The Catalan generating function C(t)=1+tC(t)², obtained either from the binomial formula or the quadratic binomial expansion, gives the asserted expression and quadratic equation. Analytic continuation determines G off the spectral interval with the chosen branch. These calculations establish properties of the measure; convergence of GUE empirical spectra to this measure is a further probabilistic theorem with an ensemble normalization and limiting argument.',
 ['Compute the first five nonzero moments and a resolvent value.','The moments are1,1,2,5,14. Atz=3, G(3)=(3−√5)/2 and satisfies G²−3G+1=0.','The measure has variance1 and fourth moment2. A standard Gaussian instead has fourth moment3, so the two laws are distinguished by an exact observable.'],
 'No finite-size Tracy–Widom law, random-matrix universality or convergence rate follows from the density calculation. Those archived labels remain separate questions.',
 'Specify the Hermitian ensemble entry variance, prove convergence of its trace moments to these Catalan numbers, and bound the nonplanar-pairing error at each fixed moment order.',
 'Middle-layer equations:')
add(3,'A frozen tangent kernel gives an exact learning dynamics with a visible nullspace',
 'What does a tangent-kernel model actually guarantee, and which labels can it never learn?',
 ['For n training observations let f(θ)=Φθ∈R^n, with fixed matrix Φ, and use loss(1/2)||f−y||². Gradient flow is θ′=−Φᵀ(Φθ−y). Put K=ΦΦᵀ.',
 'For a differentiable nonlinear model, K(θ)=J(θ)J(θ)ᵀ is its empirical tangent kernel. The fixed-matrix model is exact; replacing K(θ_t) by K(θ_0) in a nonlinear network requires an approximation theorem.'],
 'The training residual r=f−y satisfies r′=−Kr and r(t)=e^(−tK)r(0). Its component in ker K remains constant. Its component in the positive spectral subspace decays at least as e^(−λ_min^+t), where λ_min^+ is the smallest positive eigenvalue.',
 'Differentiate f along gradient flow to get f′=−ΦΦᵀ(f−y). Diagonalize the symmetric positive-semidefinite K orthogonally. Each spectral coordinate solves r_j′=−λ_jr_j, hence r_j(t)=e^(−λ_jt)r_j(0). A zero eigenvalue preserves its coordinate, while positive coordinates decay. This proves the formula and rate, and also yields loss derivative −rᵀKr≤0. For a nonlinear model the chain rule still gives f′=−K(θ_t)(f−y), but the constant-coefficient exponential solution is unavailable unless the kernel is constant or an additional commutation/approximation argument is supplied. Small training error in either model also says nothing by itself about unseen inputs. That question needs a distribution, target class and a generalization theorem.',
 ['Use Φ=(1,1)ᵀ, so K=[[1,1],[1,1]], and start θ=0.','For y=(1,1), predictions equal(1−e^(−2t))(1,1) and loss is e^(−4t). For y=(1,−1), the target lies in ker K, θ′=0 and loss stays1.','The same nonnegative kernel perfectly fits one target direction and completely misses the other. Rank and spectrum determine this finite training conclusion.'],
 'The model is an exact finite linear feature model. It does not prove a finite-width nonlinear network stays in its tangent regime or has improved out-of-sample performance.',
 'Assume a quantitative bound ||K(θ_t)−K_0||≤ε on a time interval and derive a variation-of-constants residual bound relative to the frozen model, with explicit dependence on the positive spectral gap.',
 'Middle-layer equations:',[{'label':'Jacot, Gabriel and Hongler, Neural Tangent Kernel: Convergence and Generalization in Neural Networks','url':'https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.07572'}])
add(4,'A two-point spectral distance can be computed from the commutator norm',
 'How does a specified Dirac-type operator turn algebraic observables into a distance?',
 ['Represent A=C² on H=C² by diagonal matrices diag(a_1,a_2). Let D=[[0,m],[conj(m),0]] with m≠0.',
 'For the two evaluation states δ_i, define d(δ_1,δ_2)=sup{|a_1−a_2|:a=a*,||[D,a]||≤1}. This is the distance formula in this explicitly specified finite model.'],
 'd(δ_1,δ_2)=1/|m|. If m=0, the two states have infinite distance under the same supremum.',
 'For self-adjoint a, the commutator is [[0,m(a_2−a_1)],[conj(m)(a_1−a_2),0]]. Its adjoint times itself is |m|²|a_1−a_2|²I, so its operator norm is |m||a_1−a_2|. The Lipschitz constraint is therefore precisely |a_1−a_2|≤1/|m|. This upper bound is attained by a_1=0 and a_2=1/|m|, proving the distance. If m=0 the constraint imposes no restriction on the difference, so the supremum diverges. The calculation shows exactly where a geometry enters: in the specified operator D and its interaction with the representation. An algebra alone does not determine a metric; changing |m| continuously changes the distance while leaving the algebra and its two states unchanged.',
 ['Compare two operator choices on the same algebra.','For m=2 the distance is1/2; for m=1/3 it is3. For m=0 it is infinite.','The distance formula is an exact finite optimization, with both an upper bound and an attaining observable.'],
 'This model does not establish an emergent geometry for an unspecified learned representation, or replace the analytic hypotheses of an infinite spectral triple.',
 'Use three points and a specified weighted off-diagonal D. Compute the exact commutator-norm unit ball and compare the resulting state distances with shortest-path distances on the same weighted graph.',
 'Middle-layer equations:')
add(5,'An index counts a kernel–cokernel defect in an explicit infinite model',
 'What can an index theorem be expected to measure before one invokes any geometric formula?',
 ['Let H=ℓ²(N_0) with orthonormal basis e_0,e_1,…, and let S be the unilateral shift Se_j=e_{j+1}. A bounded Fredholm operator has closed range and finite-dimensional kernel and cokernel.',
 'Its index is ind T=dim ker T−dim(H/ran T). The adjoint satisfies S*e_0=0 and S*e_{j+1}=e_j.'],
 'S is Fredholm with index−1, S* has index1, and for every k≥1, ind(S^k)=−k. In particular a finite-dimensional square matrix cannot reproduce this nonzero index.',
 'S preserves norm, so its kernel is zero and its range is the closed hyperplane of sequences with zeroth coordinate zero. Its cokernel is spanned by e_0, giving index−1. For S*, the range is all H and the kernel is span(e_0), giving index1. The kth shift has range equal to the closed subspace whose first k coordinates vanish and has no kernel, proving index−k. For a linear endomorphism T of an n-dimensional space, rank-nullity gives dim kerT=n−rankT and dim cokerT=n−rankT, so its index is zero. Truncating an infinite shift to an n-by-n matrix therefore introduces an extra kernel or boundary effect that exactly cancels the infinite cokernel defect. This is a concrete reason that a finite spectral computation requires a separate theorem before being identified with a geometric or analytic index.',
 ['Take S² and compare its n=4 truncation.','The infinite operator has kernel0 and cokernel span(e_0,e_1), so index−2. The four-by-four shift by two has kernel span(e_2,e_3) and cokernel span(e_0,e_1), so index0.','Both defects are explicitly visible; they cannot be merged into one unspecified numerical invariant.'],
 'This entry computes a Fredholm index. It does not prove the Atiyah–Singer formula or identify the index of an unspecified elliptic differential operator.',
 'Specify a Toeplitz operator with a nonvanishing Laurent-polynomial symbol and prove its index from the symbol winding, beginning with z^k and a controlled factorization.',
 'Middle-layer equations:')
add(6,'Two-dimensional sewing has a concrete Frobenius-algebra model',
 'Can topological-field-theory language be grounded in exact multiplication, pairing and gluing calculations?',
 ['Let A=Q^r have idempotent basis e_i, with e_ie_j=δ_ije_i and unit1=Σe_i. Choose nonzero rational weights w_i and counit ε(e_i)=w_i.',
 'The pairing is⟨a,b⟩=ε(ab). Its matrix is diag(w_i), hence it is nondegenerate. Define Δ(e_i)=w_i^(−1)e_i⊗e_i and handle operator H=μΔ.'],
 'These maps satisfy associativity, coassociativity, the counit laws and the Frobenius compatibility Δ(ab)=(a⊗1)Δ(b)=(1⊗b)Δ(a). The associated connected closed genus-g scalar is Z_g=ε(H^g1)=Σ_iw_i^(1−g).',
 'All identities can be checked on the idempotent basis. Multiplication is diagonal, and each coproduct has support only on e_i⊗e_i, so the two iterated coproducts both send e_i to w_i^(−2)e_i⊗e_i⊗e_i. Applying ε to either output factor of Δ returns e_i. The Frobenius identity vanishes for i≠j and equals w_i^(−1)e_i⊗e_i for i=j. Finally H(e_i)=w_i^(−1)e_i, so applying g handles to the unit and then ε gives the stated sum. These are the algebraic sewing identities behind this two-dimensional example. The general equivalence between oriented two-dimensional TQFTs and commutative Frobenius algebras is a separate structural theorem; the explicit calculations here instantiate its algebraic side rather than prove the classification.',
 ['Set r=2 and weights(w_1,w_2)=(1,2).','The sphere scalar is3, the torus scalar is2, and genus2 gives3/2. H is diag(1,1/2). Every map and each gluing identity is defined over Q.','This produces exact topological sewing data instead of an analogy based only on the name TQFT.'],
 'This is a two-dimensional commutative Frobenius model. It is not a Chern–Simons theory, a modular tensor category or a claim about quantum gravity.',
 'Replace the diagonal algebra by Q[x]/(x²), choose ε(1)=0,ε(x)=1, and compute its coproduct, nilpotent handle operator and closed-surface values. Compare the two sewing theories through their algebra invariants.',
 'Middle-layer equations:',[{'label':'Lowell Abrams, Two-dimensional topological quantum field theories and Frobenius algebras','url':'https://www.math.umd.edu/~jmr/AbramsTQFT.pdf'}])
add(7,'The recovered Picard–Fuchs operator has a checkable Frobenius recurrence',
 'What local analytic and monodromy information follows from the actual displayed second-order operator?',
 ['Fix ε∈C and consider u(1−u)y″+[1−(3+2ε)u]y′+ε(ε+1)y/4=0 near u=0.',
 'Normalize the analytic solution y_1(0)=1. Set a+b=2+2ε and ab=−ε(ε+1)/4; the equation is a Gauss hypergeometric equation with c=1.'],
 'The unique normalized analytic solution has c_0=1 and c_{n+1}=[n(n+2+2ε)−ε(ε+1)/4]c_n/(n+1)². Its radius of convergence is at least1. A second local solution is y_2=y_1 logu plus an analytic germ, giving a nontrivial unipotent local monodromy in this normalized basis.',
 'Insert Σc_nu^n and compare coefficients of u^n. The coefficient of c_{n+1} is(n+1)² and the remaining term gives the recurrence. Its ratio has limit1 unless the series terminates, proving convergence for |u|<1. Since y_1(0)=1, it is nonzero on a sufficiently small disk. Dividing the ODE by u(1−u), the coefficient of y′ is P(u)=1/u−(2+2ε)/(1−u). Reduction of order therefore supplies y_2=y_1∫[u^(−1)(1−u)^(−2−2ε)/y_1²]du. The integrand is1/u times a holomorphic function with value1 at zero, so its primitive is logu plus a holomorphic germ. A positive loop around zero sends y_2 to y_2+2πiy_1 and fixes y_1. Thus repeated Frobenius exponent0 has been strengthened to an actual local logarithm with its normalization proved, without presuming a global period interpretation.',
 ['Set ε=1.','The first coefficients are c_0=1,c_1=−1/2,c_2=−9/16,c_3=−23/32. The local monodromy fixes y_1 and adds2πiy_1 to y_2.','The recurrence and logarithmic companion follow directly from the recovered operator, even though a geometric family whose periods it governs was not recovered.'],
 'An operator label does not identify a unique algebraic family, motive, integral cycle or arithmetic monodromy group. Those geometric data remain unspecified.',
 'Recover a concrete family and differential form, derive this operator by reduction of exact forms, then identify the integral period lattice and connection matrices between0,1 and infinity.',
 'analyzed Picard–Fuchs operator')
add(8,'A certified arctangent acceleration follows from a geometric expansion',
 'Can a series-acceleration proposal be converted into an exact identity with an elementary error bound?',
 ['Use I=∫_0^1dx/(1+x²)=π/4 and the identity1/(1+x²)=(1/2)/(1−(1−x²)/2). Let J_k=∫_0^1(1−x²)^kdx.',
 'All geometric expansion terms are nonnegative and their ratio is at most1/2 on[0,1], so uniform convergence justifies termwise integration.'],
 'π/4=Σ_{k≥0}2^(k−1)(k!)²/(2k+1)!. The truncation after k=0,…,N−1 has positive error at most2^(−N). The terms satisfy t_{k+1}/t_k=(k+1)/(2k+3).',
 'Expand the denominator geometrically and integrate to get I=ΣJ_k/2^(k+1). To evaluate J_k, integrate the derivative of x(1−x²)^(k+1), whose boundary values vanish. This gives J_{k+1}=2(k+1)∫x²(1−x²)^kdx=2(k+1)(J_k−J_{k+1}), so J_{k+1}=2(k+1)J_k/(2k+3). Starting with J_0=1 yields J_k=2^(2k)(k!)²/(2k+1)!. Substitution gives the series and its term recurrence. The geometric remainder under the integral is [((1−x²)/2)^N]/(1+x²), which is between0 and2^(−N). Integrating over a unit interval proves the error bound. This supplies an identity, a recurrence certificate and a stopping criterion, rather than a floating agreement with π.',
 ['Use N=20.','The rational partial sum S_20 satisfies0<π−4S_20≤2^(−18). In comparison, the alternating arctangent series after20 terms only has its elementary error bound4/41.','The acceleration has an exponentially decreasing certified error, derived without assuming cancellation in a floating sum.'],
 'The result is one explicit acceleration motivated by the recovered Kerala/summation-by-parts topic. It is not a recovered historical Kerala formula or a proof that every proposed Ore/WZ identity is valid.',
 'Build a general certificate from a rational integral with a uniformly bounded geometric ratio and optimize its splitting point to minimize a rigorously bounded arithmetic cost.',
 'connected Kerala series acceleration')
add(9,'A factorial series has a fully visible Borel singularity and Stokes jump',
 'What does resurgence mean in a model where the divergent coefficients, Borel pole and exponentially small ambiguity can all be written down?',
 ['Consider the formal series F(z)=Σ_{n≥0}n!z^(n+1), which has radius zero by the ratio test. Its formal Borel transform is B(t)=Σt^n=1/(1−t).',
 'For positive real z, define lateral Laplace integrals F_±(z)=∫_{γ_±}e^(−t/z)/(1−t)dt, where γ_± follows the positive ray and bypasses t=1 above or below. Endpoint decay and the simple indentation define the limits.'],
 'The lateral sums have the same factorial asymptotic expansion and satisfy z²F′−F=−z. With the stated above-minus-below contour convention, F_+−F_−=2πie^(−1/z). Every difference of two solutions of the ODE is Ce^(−1/z).',
 'For the positive-ray asymptotics, split each fixed bypass contour at0<a<1. The local part admits the geometric expansion with its integral remainder; the remaining contour has real part bounded away from zero and contributes an exponentially small term asz↓0. The formal ODE follows by differentiating the series: z²F′=F−z. For the Laplace integrals it follows by differentiation and integration by parts, with endpoints controlled by exponential decay and the matched contour bypass. Their difference is a closed clockwise contour around the pole. The residue of e^(−t/z)/(1−t) is−e^(−1/z), so clockwise residue evaluation gives2πie^(−1/z). The homogeneous ODE is H′=H/z², whose solutions are Ce^(−1/z), agreeing with the contour calculation. Along the negative ray write z=−a,a>0 and obtain F(−a)=−∫_0^∞e^(−u/a)/(1+u)du. Expanding1/(1+u) through degree N−1 with its exact remainder shows that the error after N factorial terms has magnitude at most N!a^(N+1). This proves an asymptotic statement even though the infinite power series diverges at every nonzero z.',
 ['Take a=1/10 on the nonsingular negative ray and N=5.','The remainder magnitude is at most5!(1/10)^6=0.00012. Across the positive Stokes ray the lateral ambiguity is exactly2πie^(−10).','The divergent series is useful to finite order, and its missing exponentially small datum is made explicit by the Borel pole.'],
 'This is a solvable one-pole model. It does not determine any Stokes constant for an unspecified Feynman amplitude, knot state integral or motivic period.',
 'Replace1/(1−t) by a specified rational Borel transform with several poles and derive all lateral jumps and an optimal truncation estimate uniformly on closed sectors avoiding their rays.',
 'Additional transcendental topics:')
add(10,'A triangular regular-singular connection encodes a dilogarithm period',
 'How does an iterated integral become an entry of a specified differential system, rather than merely an analogy with a KZ connection?',
 ['Let E_ij denote the three-by-three matrix unit. Set A=E_12,B=E_23 and consider y′=[A/z+B/(1−z)]y on0<z<1, with the analytic solution y(0)=(0,0,1).',
 'Define Li₂(z)=Σ_{n≥1}z^n/n² for|z|<1. The matrices do not commute: [A,B]=E_13. This is a specified triangular regular-singular system, not a recovered full Drinfeld associator.'],
 'The solution is y(z)=(Li₂(z),−log(1−z),1). Its finite endpoint first coordinate is Li₂(1)=ζ(2)=π²/6, obtained from the ordered integral∫_0^1[∫_0^z dt/(1−t)]dz/z.',
 'The third coordinate is constant. The second therefore integrates to−log(1−z). Dividing it byz gives the derivative of the first coordinate, and termwise integration of the absolutely convergent logarithmic series produces Li₂(z). Monotone convergence atz=1 givesΣ1/n²; convergence also follows directly from the integral test. For completeness, the periodic Bernoulli polynomial B₂(x)=x²−x+1/6 has mean zero and, by two integrations by parts, nonzero Fourier coefficients1/(2π²n²). Their absolute convergence gives B₂(0)=Σ_{n≥1}1/(π²n²), provingζ(2)=π²/6. The ordered integral corresponds to first applying the B-channel and then the A-channel; their product AB=E_13 is nonzero while BA=0. Thus ordering has an exact algebraic effect. Higher iterated integrals can encode multiple-zeta values, but a general associator requires tangential-basepoint regularization and all of its compatibility identities, none of which is supplied by this one finite example.',
 ['Truncate Li₂(1) afterN terms, and inspect the two channel products.','The remainder lies between1/(N+1) and1/N. AB=E_13 andBA=0. AtN=100 the rational sum gives a rigorous interval of width1/10100 containingπ²/6.','The endpoint period, a noncommuting channel order and an explicit error bound coexist in a complete small model.'],
 'The vector solution has a divergent second coordinate at1 but a finite first coordinate. It does not define a full matrix holonomy from0to1 without the appropriate endpoint normalization, or prove general MZV independence.',
 'Construct a longer nilpotent matrix chain for a specified convergent word in dz/z and dz/(1−z), then derive its endpoint multiple-zeta value and demonstrate the shuffle identity by decomposing a product integration region.',
 'Additional transcendental topics:')
(P/'catalog_entries.json').write_text(json.dumps(entries,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
